import json
A = {}
A["stage1/problem/A1/v1"] = """Problem: Can MVFA's segmentation adapters be trained without pixel masks, using pseudo-masks from labelled abnormal source images only (a patch of an abnormal image is marked anomalous when its frozen CLIP level-4 feature lies farther from every patch of the same dataset's normal images than the 99th percentile of normal-to-normal nearest-neighbour distances on source validation data), and how much of the zero-shot pixel AUROC and Dice of the mask-supervised MVFA does this recover on a held-out modality, as masks are removed from zero, one, or all three masked source datasets?

Rationale: MVFA's anomaly maps come only from the segmentation adapters, which are trained with Dice and Focal losses on masks of BrainMRI, LiverCT and RESC (G3); the three image-only datasets (OCT17, ChestXray, HIS) never supervise localisation. The question is what dense expert masks actually buy. Unlike CoDeGraph [30], which derives pseudo-masks from unlabelled batches and must first filter consistent anomalies, here image-level labels veto false positives: pseudo-masks are drawn only from images known to be abnormal and are defined against normal images of the same dataset, with a threshold fixed on source data. DDAD [54], MediCLIP [10] and SpatioPrompt [20] use unlabelled or synthetic signals in other frameworks; none tests MVFA's own multi-level adapters. The answer is reported as a label-cost curve (masks on three, one or zero sources, plus the three image-only datasets added as pseudo-mask sources), and pseudo-mask quality is first measured against true masks on the three masked datasets (IoU, pixel AUROC) so that a negative result can be attributed. One loss target of one branch changes; pseudo-masks are computed once with frozen features, so the cost equals MVFA's training under the shared leave-one-modality-out protocol."""

A["stage1/problem/A2/v1"] = """Problem: When MVFA's few-shot adaptation receives only K normal support images (no abnormal images, no masks), should synthesised lesions supervise only the segmentation adapters, only the detection adapters, or both, and how much of the image- and pixel-level AUROC of the standard K-normal + K-masked-abnormal protocol does the best normal-only variant retain for K in {2, 4, 8, 16} over three support draws?

Rationale: The released code defines 'K-shot' as K normal plus K abnormal images with masks (G4), so MVFA's few-shot numbers assume the expensive part of clinical labelling, and the segmentation branch has no other route to supervision (G3). MediCLIP [10] and SpatioPrompt [20] show that CLIP can be adapted from normal images with anomaly synthesis, but in single-branch designs; MVFA separates detection and segmentation adapters at four levels, and synthetic lesions are known to transfer imperfectly to real pathology, so which branch should consume synthetic supervision is an open design question specific to the target method. Synthesis follows MediCLIP's published recipe (fixed families and parameters), so the only variable is where the synthetic supervision is applied. The outcome answers a practical question, whether a clinic needs any annotated abnormal studies to adapt MVFA, and the three-draw protocol also answers the single-draw part of G4. Few-shot fine-tuning takes minutes per run on one GPU."""

A["stage1/problem/B1/v1"] = """Problem: How far do MVFA's published few-shot results depend on transductive test-set min-max normalisation, and can an inductive per-image score, in which each branch is standardised with statistics of the K normal support images and source-validation data, recover image AUROC within 1 point while making each image's score invariant to the rest of the test set? Changes are evaluated in a fixed order: normalisation first, then fusion weight, then image-level aggregation, with sensitivity at 95% specificity and expected calibration error reported alongside AUROC.

Rationale: The released code normalises each branch with the minimum and maximum over the entire test set before fusing with weight 0.5 (G8), takes the image label from the maximum of the fused map (G9), and reports only AUC (G11). A deployed detector scores one image at a time, so the published numbers are not those of a usable system, and the size of this gap has never been measured. Inductive normalisation with validation statistics is common in anomaly-detection tooling, and CANOR [55] calibrates CLIP scores with a handful of scalars, but no medical CLIP detector in the review set (LaRP-CLIP [17], HeadCLIP [4]) measures the effect of transductive normalisation on reported results. The headline measurement is new: the shift of an individual image's score when the rest of the test set is resampled (prevalence 10-50%, with and without outliers), for the original and the inductive pipeline. The ordered ablation attributes any change to one component at a time. The study is inference-only on retrained checkpoints and takes hours on one GPU."""

A["stage1/problem/B2/v1"] = """Problem: Does the best image-level pooling of MVFA's anomaly maps depend on lesion size? Comparing max (paper, Eq. 3), mean (released training code), top-k mean with k tied to the source datasets' median lesion area, and noisy-OR, each applied identically in training and testing, which gives the highest image AUROC per held-out modality, and how does each degrade when spurious high-scoring pixels mimicking bright vessels or acquisition noise are injected into normal test maps?

Rationale: The paper and the released code disagree on pooling (G17), and the test-time image score on masked datasets is the maximum of the fused map, so one spurious pixel can decide the label (G9); robustness is never measured (G11). CANOR [55] shows that noisy-OR beats naive max/mean aggregation for CLIP chest X-ray scores, and LaRP-CLIP [17] and HeadCLIP [4] build image scores from pixel evidence, but none relates the choice to lesion size, which differs by an order of magnitude across BMAD (small brain lesions, larger liver tumours, diffuse retinal fluid). The hypothesis is that max suits small lesions and top-k or noisy-OR suit large or diffuse ones. Each variant is a one-line change in training and testing, so four retrainings suffice; results are stratified by lesion-size tertiles on the masked datasets."""

A["stage1/problem/D1/v1"] = """Problem: Are MVFA's adapted patch features compact enough that a per-level Gaussian with shrinkage covariance, or 32 k-means prototypes, fitted on the K normal support images can replace the few-shot memory bank of raw patch features without losing more than 1 point of pixel or image AUROC, and how do memory, latency and the information stored about patients scale with K (2-64) and input resolution (240 and 448) for each option?

Rationale: MVFA compares query patches with a memory bank that grows linearly with K and resolution, stores raw patient-derived features, and never models the compact distribution of normal tissue explicitly (G7, G13). Gaussian normal models (PaDiM) and compact prototypes (Spatial-FAD [6]) work for other features, and AVA-DINO [14] argues that normals are compact and anomalies diverse; whether MVFA's adapted features have this property has not been measured. The study first tests Gaussianity of adapted versus raw CLIP features per level, which explains when a compact model suffices, then compares the three options in a regime where the bank's cost matters (K up to 64, 448-pixel input). A parametric model stores statistics rather than raw features, which is the privacy benefit G13 asks about. No retraining is needed; each fit takes seconds."""

A["stage1/problem/D2/v1"] = """Problem: Do prompts learned on source modalities transfer to an unseen modality inside MVFA? With MVFA's visual adapters frozen at their trained checkpoint, learnable normal/abnormal prompt tokens (16 tokens per state, optionally conditioned on the mean adapted patch feature) are trained on the source modalities and compared with the fixed 35-template prompts on the held-out modality, measuring zero-shot AUROC and the spread of AUROC across ten alternative prompt wordings.

Rationale: MVFA assumes a single universal text notion of normal and abnormal across modalities and never analyses prompt sensitivity (G5). Learnable and visually conditioned prompts are common in the review set (AnomalyCLIP [11], AdaCLIP [32], CoPS [41], VCF-CLIP [22], IQE-CLIP [7]), and medical prompt studies report unstable performance across wordings [50], but none isolates cross-modality transfer of learned prompts under a modality-disjoint split, which is the setting MVFA claims. Freezing the visual adapters isolates the text side and keeps training cheap. The main outcome is the reduction in wording sensitivity; the AUROC change on held-out modalities is secondary."""
json.dump(A, open("logs/answers/run2/s1_v1.json", "w"), indent=1)
